<div class="prose max-w-prose">

Good web design is often described as something that feels effortless, but the
reality is that effortlessness is the result of careful thinking, structure,
and accessibility. When a website works well, users should not need to stop and
question how to navigate it. The experience should feel intuitive, clear, and
confident from the moment they arrive.

I wrote this article as a Product Designer with a focus on how design should
reduce friction rather than create it. In my work across print, maritime,
SaaS, and digital services, I have seen how strong design is not just about
visual polish — it is about clarity, usability, and making complex information
easy to understand.

## Why good design feels invisible

The best digital experiences are often the ones users barely notice. They do
not have to think about why a button is placed where it is, why content is laid
out in a certain way, or why a process feels smooth and logical. That feeling
comes from deliberate design decisions: strong hierarchy, helpful navigation,
accessible contrast, and attention to how people actually behave.

When design fails, it creates friction. That friction may be obvious, such as a
confusing layout or poor mobile experience, or it may be subtle, such as
unclear messaging or inaccessible interactions. In either case, it slows people
down and weakens trust.

## Accessibility is essential, not optional

Accessibility is a core part of good design. A website that is visually strong
but difficult to use for some people is not truly successful. Thoughtful design
means creating experiences that work for a wide range of users, including those
using screen readers, keyboard navigation, and assistive technologies.

Accessible design is not a separate layer added at the end — it is part of the
foundation of good user experience. It helps create clearer layouts, stronger
content structure, and interfaces that are easier for everyone to use.

## Design across industries

My approach to digital design has been shaped by working across different
industries and business types. Each environment presents a slightly different
challenge: some need clarity and conversion, others need trust and authority,
and some need to communicate technical information without overwhelming the user.

That range has reinforced a simple design principle for me: the best digital
products make complex information easier to understand without reducing its
importance.

## Writing as an expert in the field

This article was published in the Aberdeen Chamber of Commerce magazine and on
the Intellicore blog, where I shared my perspective on what makes web design
work in practice. It reflects both my professional experience and my broader
belief that design should be useful, inclusive, and quietly effective.

Good design does not need to shout. It works best when it feels obvious,
confident, and easy to use.

